# xv6 Demand Paging Implementation

> **Languages:** C, RISC-V Assembly
> **Platform:** xv6 (RISC-V)

## Overview

I enhanced the xv6 operating system to support **POSIX-style memory-mapped files (`mmap`/`munmap`) with demand paging**.

This project was motivated by a desire to understand **how lazy page allocation works in modern operating systems**, and how page faults, virtual memory areas, and file-backed mappings interact inside the kernel.

Rather than eagerly loading file data into memory, the system now **allocates pages on first access**, improving performance and memory efficiency.

## Results

| Mapping Size | Pages Touched | Lazy Paging | Eager Paging |
|--------------|---------------|-------------|--------------|
| 64 pages (256 KB) | 8 pages | 1 tick | 3 ticks |
| 64 pages (256 KB) | 32 pages | 2 ticks | 3 ticks |

**Result:** Lazy paging achieved up to **3× lower latency** for sparse-access workloads.
